.TH ld "" "Command"
.SH NAME
ld \- load/bind relocatable object files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBld [\fIoption ...\fB] \fIfile ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
ld -- load/bind relocatable object files
Usage:	ld [ option ... ] file ...
Options:
	-d	Define commons (even if undefined symbols)
	-e ent	Set entry point to given symbol or octal number
	-i	Bind output sepid
	-k[sys]	Kernel process; use system symbol table (default: '/coherent')
	-l<lib>	Use standard library <lib>
	-m	Perform in-memory load if possible (larger but faster)
	-n	Bind output shared
	-o file	Place output in named file (default: 'l.out')
	-r	Retain relocation information
	-s	Discard symbol table
	-u sym	Undefine 'sym' (forcing library search)
	-X	Discard C internal local symbols
	-x	Discard all local symbols
.HE
.B ld
combines relocatable object files, produced by
.BR cc ", " as ", or " ld ,
with routines drawn from library archives produced by
.BR ar ,
to build an executable (or further relocatable) output file.
.PP
.B ld
scans its arguments left to right.
Each non-option argument is either a relocatable object file or a
library archive.
A library archive is searched only to resolve undefined references;
if there are none outstanding,
.B ld
goes on to the next argument immediately.
The library is scanned from first module to last, repeatedly, until a
whole pass adds nothing further to the output; a module is bound in
as soon as it resolves an outstanding undefined symbol.
.PP
The following options are recognized:
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Define common symbols in the output even if undefined symbols remain.
Normally commons are defined only when the link is otherwise
complete (no undefined symbols, or relocatable output).
.IP "\fB\-e\fI ent\fR"
Set the entry point of the output to
.IR ent ,
given either as a symbol name or as a number (a leading
.B 0
means octal,
.B 0x
means hexadecimal).
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Bind the output with separated (sepid) text and data.
.IP \fB\-k\fI[sys]\fR
Link a kernel process or loadable driver: read the symbol table of
.I sys
(default
.BR /coherent )
to resolve references against the running kernel, and mark the output
as a kernel image.
.IP "\fB\-l\fIlib\fR"
An abbreviation for the library
\fB/lib/lib\fIlib\fB.a\fR,
or
\fB/usr/lib/lib\fIlib\fB.a\fR
if the first is not found.
.IP \fB\-L\fR
Build large-model output: relax the check that a segment's address
range fits within the machine's normal per-segment size limit.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
Build the output image in memory, when it is small enough to fit,
instead of seeking a disk file for each segment.
Larger memory use, faster link.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Bind the output as a shared (pure) text image.
.IP "\fB\-o \fIfile\fR"
Write the output into
.I file
instead of the default,
.BR l.out .
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Retain relocation information in the output and do not complain about
undefined symbols; produces a
.B .o
file as if its pieces had been compiled together.
.IP "\fB\-R \fIbase\fR"
Set the relocation base of the output to
.I base
(a symbol name or a number) instead of the machine's usual default.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Discard the symbol table from the output.
.IP "\fB\-u \fIsym\fR"
Add
.I sym
to the symbol table as an undefined global reference, to force a
library search that pulls in whichever module defines it --
for example,
.BR "\-u _dtoa_" ,
to force the real floating-point formatter ahead of its stub in
.BR libc.a .
.IP \fB\-w\fR
Watch: print progress messages (module and undefined-symbol counts,
final image size) as linking proceeds.
This option is accepted by the program but is not listed in the
Options table above.
.IP \fB\-X\fR
Discard C-internal local symbols.
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Discard all local symbols.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fBl.out\fR	default output file
\fB/coherent\fR	default kernel symbol table for \fB\-k\fR
\fB/lib/lib*.a\fR	standard libraries
\fB/usr/lib/lib*.a\fR	more libraries
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ar ,
.BR as ,
.B cc
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.IP "no input found"
No relocatable object file was read; there is nothing to link.
.IP "cannot open \fIfile\fR"
.B ld
prints
.B "can't open \fIfile\fR"
and exits: it could not open the named object file or library.
.IP "cannot create \fIfile\fR"
.B ld
could not create the output file named by
.B \-o
(or
.BR l.out ).
.IP "segment larger than \fIn\fRKb"
A segment of the output exceeds the machine's normal size limit for a
segment; relink with
.B \-L
if the output is genuinely large-model.
.IP "address wraparound"
A segment's address computation overflowed; the output would not run
correctly.
Printed with the added hint ``use `\-L''' when the wraparound would be
cured by large-model output.
.SH NOTES
When linking a program by hand (running
.B ld
independently of
.BR cc ),
be sure to include the appropriate run-time start-up object
(such as
.BR crts0.o )
on the command line; otherwise the program will not link correctly.
